🚀 Silver Layer Data Pipeline (Phase I)
(Phase II......)

This project is designed to simulate how data actually flows inside a real-world data engineering environment.
Most data projects focus on writing queries or building a few dashboards.

Instead of treating SQL as just a querying tool, this implementation builds a structured, modular, and fully traceable data pipeline, closely resembling production-grade ETL systems.

At its core, this project implements a Silver Layer architecture using the AdventureWorks dataset.

The goal is to transform raw, domain-level data into a clean, structured, and analysis-ready format while maintaining full control over execution, monitoring, and data movement.

The design emphasizes not just transformation, but also:

Observability
Reliability
Scalability

The pipeline begins with domain-specific views across multiple business areas such as:

Sales
Production
Purchasing
Human Resources
Person

These views act as a controlled abstraction layer, ensuring that the upstream data remains decoupled from transformation logic.

From there, data flows into Input (staging) tables, which serve as an intermediate checkpoint.

This staging layer allows:

Safe reprocessing
Easier debugging
Data validation
Isolation from source systems

Once staged, the data is processed through modular stored procedures, each dedicated to a specific dataset.

These procedures:

Handle transformations
Load data into Output tables
Track operational metrics

The processed data is then stored in Output tables, which represent the final Silver Layer.

The current implementation follows an idempotent Delete + Insert pattern, ensuring consistent and repeatable results across executions.

Each procedure also captures:

Rows inserted
Rows deleted

This provides transparency into how data changes during each run.

A key highlight of this project is the orchestration and logging framework.

A main stored procedure controls the execution flow by triggering all child procedures in a defined sequence.

At the same time, a structured logging system captures both session-level and execution-level details, including:

Start time
End time
Execution status
Row counts
Error messages

This creates a complete audit trail, making it easier to monitor runs and debug failures — similar to real production environments.

What makes this project stand out is its focus on pipeline thinking rather than isolated transformations.

It is designed to be:

Modular
Restartable
Traceable

This allows individual components to be tested, rerun, or extended independently.

This is Phase I of the project, focused on building a strong foundation for structured data pipelines.

Future enhancements may include:

Incremental loading (CDC)
MERGE-based transformations
Parallel execution
Integration with tools like ADF or Airflow
Data quality checks

If you're exploring data engineering beyond basic SQL, this project is a practical example of how to think in terms of:

👉 Systems
👉 Pipelines
👉 Production-ready design

instead of just queries.
